Shoals.Dupire.du_cubic_log_moneyness_interp: grid times must be strictly increasing: index 2 has time 0.5, which does not exceed time 0.75 at index 1

The time axis is read through `Nautilus.Interpolation.linear_interp_sorted`,
which folds over the pairs tracking the previous time and takes the first
bracket straddling the query. Out of order it bracketed (0.25, 0.20) against
(0.75, 0.45) rather than (0.25, 0.20) against (0.5, 0.40): the query at
t=0.375 returned 0.2625 where 0.30 is correct. That is a 12.5% relative error
on an implied vol, with no trap, no NaN and no diagnostic, and it propagates
through `du_local_vol_from_iv_surface` into anything priced off the surface.

The module imports `linear_interp_sorted` by name, but a precondition stated
in a dependency's identifier is a hint to a reader of `src/dupire.ch`, not a
disclosure to a caller of `du_cubic_log_moneyness_interp`, whose signature
names no ordering rule for either axis.

The fixture's rows are constant in strike and the levels 0.20 / 0.40 / 0.45
are non-collinear in time on purpose. A surface flat or affine along the time
axis interpolates to the same value for every bracket the code could pick, so
it cannot distinguish this defect from correct behaviour -- which is why
`tests/dupire.ch`'s own grid, built from the column index alone and therefore
constant in time, passed throughout. The paired positive fixture varies
non-linearly in both axes.
